## Original Content
<img src="https://assetsio.gnwcdn.com/optimisim-week-side-projects.png?width=690&amp;quality=85&amp;format=jpg&amp;auto=webp" /> <p>If there's been one theme I'd pick out from the many of these past few years of gaming discontent, it's the sense of all-out assault on creativity. This is the thing that makes games what they are, that makes them successful, that earns people money and gives people a reason to do the job in the first place, but it's also the thing that seems to be valued least at so many of those making the decisions. To those people creativity is something to be funneled, moulded, directed or strictly defined. Often, I suspect, it's just not really something directly thought about at all. At best it's a nice buzzword to roll out as needed, but rare one that's even remotely understood.</p> <p><a href="https://www.eurogamer.net/optimism-week-games-industry-side-projects-creativity">Read more</a></p>

## Summary
The gaming industry often undervalues creativity, treating it as a buzzword rather than a core asset. Game developers' side projects showcase unbridled creativity, contrasting with the industry's restrictive approach. This highlights the importance of nurturing creativity in game development.

## Key Points
- The gaming industry often undervalues creativity
- Side projects by game devs demonstrate unbridled creativity
- Industry's approach to creativity is often restrictive


